from __future__ import annotations

from thinking_core import render_long, render_note


def stop_ai_use_case() -> str:
    body = '''<p>One of the hardest decisions in an AI portfolio is not which idea to start. It is deciding when an initiative has learned enough and should stop.</p><p>Organizations are usually better at generating AI ideas than removing them. Once a use case has a sponsor, a team, a prototype and some visible momentum, stopping can feel like admitting failure. The result is predictable: pilots stay alive, teams keep refining them, and weak initiatives continue consuming attention because nobody wants to be the person who closes them.</p><blockquote>Stopping an AI use case is not evidence that the portfolio failed. Keeping weak work alive after the evidence changed is.</blockquote><h2>The danger is not experimentation. It is zombie experimentation.</h2><p>Early AI work should contain uncertainty. That is the point. A discovery or pilot phase exists to test whether the problem is worth solving, whether AI materially improves the situation, whether the required data and workflow conditions are realistic and whether users will actually change how they work.</p><p>The mistake is treating every positive technical result as an argument for continuation. A model can perform well and still support a weak business case. A prototype can impress stakeholders and still require more process change than the value justifies. A use case can be useful and still lose to another opportunity competing for the same people, data or investment.</p><p>I want experiments to create evidence, including evidence that says <strong>do not continue</strong>.</p><h2>The trade-off: stopping too early versus protecting sunk cost</h2><p>Stopping too quickly is also dangerous. AI initiatives often need iteration before the real workflow, data or user problem becomes visible. Kill every imperfect first attempt and the organization never learns.</p><p>But there is a difference between iteration that reduces uncertainty and iteration that simply extends the life of the project. I look for whether each next step is testing a material assumption. If another sprint cannot realistically change the investment decision, continuing it is difficult to justify.</p><h2>Six signals that make me question whether a use case should continue</h2><p><strong>1. The problem is no longer important enough.</strong><br>Priorities change. A use case that made sense six months ago may no longer deserve scarce AI capacity simply because the original business problem has moved down the agenda.</p><p><strong>2. The value mechanism remains vague.</strong><br>If the team still cannot explain which decision, workflow or behavior changes and how that change creates value, more model refinement may not solve the real problem.</p><p><strong>3. The operating conditions are unrealistic.</strong><br>A pilot may depend on data access, manual preparation, exceptional user attention or specialist support that cannot be sustained in ordinary operations.</p><p><strong>4. Adoption requires more change than the outcome justifies.</strong><br>Some solutions are technically credible but ask users to change so much of their work that the adoption cost overwhelms the expected benefit.</p><p><strong>5. Risk or control requirements change the economics.</strong><br>A use case may remain technically possible while the monitoring, assurance, human oversight or regulatory burden required for responsible use makes it a poor investment.</p><p><strong>6. A stronger opportunity now exists.</strong><br>Portfolio decisions are relative. A use case does not need to become bad before a better use of the same resources appears.</p><h2>Do not ask only “does it work?”</h2><p>The more useful question is whether the evidence still supports the next commitment.</p><p>At each stage I would ask: what uncertainty were we trying to reduce, what did we learn, what changed in our confidence, and what would we need to believe to justify more time, money or organizational change?</p><p>This creates three legitimate outcomes: continue, change direction, or stop. Treating continuation as the default quietly turns experimentation into accumulation.</p><h2>A stop decision should leave something behind</h2><p>Stopping does not mean deleting the work and forgetting it. A well-run portfolio captures why the initiative stopped: which assumption failed, what was learned about the data, workflow, users or technology, and whether any component is reusable elsewhere.</p><p>That matters because institutional learning is part of the return on experimentation. The organization should not have to rediscover the same constraint six months later under a different use-case name.</p><div class="rule"><strong>My rule: every AI initiative should know what evidence would cause us to stop before we become emotionally invested in continuing.</strong><p>Good portfolio leadership is not measured by how many AI initiatives survive. It is measured by whether scarce attention keeps moving toward the work with the strongest evidence and the clearest path to value.</p></div>'''
    aside = '''<span>Stop / continue check</span><strong>Six reasons to reopen the investment decision.</strong><div class="article-questions"><div>The problem lost strategic importance</div><div>The value mechanism is still weak</div><div>Production conditions are unrealistic</div><div>Adoption cost overwhelms the benefit</div><div>Risk requirements change the economics</div><div>A better opportunity now competes for the same resources</div></div><p>The point is not to kill experiments quickly. It is to make continuation an evidence-based decision rather than the default.</p>'''
    return render_long(
        "Field note · AI Portfolio & Value",
        "When an AI Use Case Should Be Stopped",
        "AI portfolios need explicit exit criteria. A technically credible use case can still be the wrong place for the next unit of investment.",
        "17 Sep 2026 · 5 min read",
        body,
        aside,
    )


def roi_diagnosed_too_late() -> str:
    return render_note(
        "Decision note · AI Value",
        "AI ROI is often diagnosed too late.",
        "If value only becomes a serious question after deployment, the portfolio has already missed the most useful moment to shape it.",
        "17 Sep 2026 · 1 min read",
        "ROI starts with the value mechanism, not the post-launch dashboard.",
        "I do not want the first serious ROI conversation to happen after an AI solution is live. By then, the team may know model performance, usage and delivery cost but still be unable to explain what business behavior was supposed to change. I prefer to define the chain early: baseline → changed decision or workflow → observable adoption → operational effect → business outcome. The numbers will mature as evidence develops, but the causal logic should exist before major investment. If we cannot describe how the use case is expected to create value at discovery stage, measuring more things after launch will not repair the missing business case.",
    )
